1.Install Julia v 1.11.1 (not other version) :

1.1 Open Ubuntu terminal ( use it for all the steps below )

1.2 Run the following commands:
wget https://julialang-s3.julialang.org/bin/linux/x64/1.11/julia-1.11.1-linux-x86_64.tar.gz
tar -xvzf julia-1.11.1-linux-x86_64.tar.gz
sudo mv julia-1.11.1 /opt/

1.3 Add Julia to PATH:
echo 'export PATH=/opt/julia-1.11.1/bin:$PATH' >> ~/.bashrc
source ~/.bashrc

1.4 Verify:
julia --version  #you should see "julia version 1.11.1"

2. Download Baysor source (USE UBUNTU TERMINAL):
cd ~
curl -L -O https://github.com/kharchenkolab/Baysor/archive/refs/heads/master.zip
unzip master.zip
cd Baysor-master
~/Baysor-master


6.Initialize Julia project (Baysor)
#From Ubuntu start Julia :
julia
using Pkg
Pkg.add("PackageCompiler")
Pkg.activate(".")
Pkg.instantiate()
exit()

7.Build Baysor binary (Ubuntu terminal NOT Julia REPL)
julia --project=. deps/build.jl  #it can take up to 1 hour

8.Add Baysor to PATH since the standard commands are not working (Ubuntu terminal)
echo 'export PATH=~/Baysor-master/bin:$PATH' >> ~/.bashrc
source ~/.bashrc

9.Verify:
baysor --help #or ~ /.julia/bin/baysor --help
#inside Baysor-master/bin there is no bin CLI because it is used "Comonicon" which installs the CLI into Julia's global bin directory. Indeed, the command run from ubuntu : "ls ~ /.julia/bin" gives as output "baysor".
Then " ~ /.julia/bin/baysor --help" outputs " baysor v0.7.1" with all the info regarding the application. This demonstrates that baysor is installed and the CLI is at /home/risti/.julia/bin/baysor.

